+# Agent Note: Planning Code Mode child tools before dispatch
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-20-code-mode-planned-child-previews.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The [nested Code Mode row design](2026-07-26-code-mode-chat-subcall-rows.md) derives each child from `tool/code-dispatch-start`, which is emitted only when the generated program invokes its binding. A program can contain a large `write` payload or substantial computation before that invocation, so the parent Code row remains the only visible activity for seconds. Users cannot distinguish that interval from a stalled harness. Starting a child timer before runtime dispatch would fill the visual gap but misstate execution time.
+
+## Decision
+
+The public `run_code` schema requires `plannedTools` before `code` and `description`. It is an ordered array of tool wire names, including one entry for every repeated call; the TypeScript and Python prompt instructions require the same order. The internal validator accepts its absence for recorded pre-DSH-015 calls, while every newly assembled model schema advertises it as required.
+
+The Web conversation projection incrementally reads complete string members from `plannedTools` as the argument JSON streams. Each completed name immediately creates a provisional child row. A call with no usable declaration falls back to a conservative scan of the streamed program for direct `tools.name(...)` and `tools["name"](...)` calls; quoted strings, template literals, Python triple-quoted strings, line comments, and block comments do not produce previews. The parser never exposes incomplete arguments to the renderer.
+
+A provisional child uses the runtime ordinal identity `<parent>:code:<n>`, shows the ordinary tool title and icon with the running sweep and `ing`, and has no start time or timer. The matching `tool/code-dispatch-start` consumes that ordinal, installs the actual running block in the same position, and starts elapsed time from the event timestamp. A mismatched declared name is replaced by the actual dispatched name; undeclared dynamic calls append normally. Plans that never dispatch disappear when the parent settles, the step ends, or the model retries.
+
+`plannedTools` is an intent hint rather than a new execution protocol. Dispatch events remain authoritative for arguments, timing, results, nesting, and durable reconstruction; no provisional row enters the Session event log or the details panel. This preserves the [live parallel-dispatch timing contract](2026-07-26-code-mode-live-parallel-dispatch.md).
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Scan only the generated program.** Rejected because a tool call after a large literal is not visible until the model has already emitted that literal, which leaves the original delay intact. Static scanning also cannot reliably resolve aliases, computed access, or dynamic control flow.
+
+**Start the child timer when its name is predicted.** Rejected because model generation time is not tool execution time. A planned row therefore carries activity state without a timestamp; only dispatch starts the timer.
+
+**Persist synthetic child-start events during model generation.** Rejected because a declared branch may never run and a model may revise its arguments before the formal call. Ephemeral conversation projection can discard unused intent without falsifying the durable transcript.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Code Mode gives immediate, tool-specific feedback before long programs reach their first dispatch, while completed rows retain millisecond precision from actual execution. Model requests carry one additional ordered string array, and the model can still omit or misstate it; legacy validation and conservative scanning keep those cases usable, but indirect or computed calls may remain undiscoverable until dispatch. Conversation-node tests pin partial streaming, ordinal replacement, mismatch handling, cleanup, and old-call scanning; Tool UI tests pin the timerless `ing` preview; Code Mode and snapshot suites pin the required public schema and legacy replay compatibility.
